Products designed without considering EPR obligations often become unrecyclable due to material complexity and contamination risks. Designing for EPR compliance from inception enables efficient material recovery. Business models internalizing end-of-life costs create incentives for recyclable design rather than externalizing environmental costs.

EPR compliance creates regulatory certainty and prevents future liability from unrecycled products in landfills. EPR-enabled business models enable manufacturers to participate in secondary material markets, creating revenue from recovered materials. Transparent EPR models build brand credibility with environmentally conscious consumers.